<header><p class="st-kicker">imperial city · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Free Imperial City of Ulm</h1><p class="st-meta">1274 CE – 1802 CE (approx.) · capital Ulm</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Danube imperial city of fustian weavers and merchants, builder of the minster with the world's tallest church spire and a leader of the Swabian League.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Ulm grew around a royal palace at the meeting of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/blau-river">Blau</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a>, recorded from 854. It was a favoured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hohenstaufen">Hohenstaufen</a> town, and after their fall it was confirmed as an imperial city, its privileges renewed by Rudolf I in 1274.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/schworbrief">Schwörbrief</a> of 1397 gave the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guild">guilds</a> a majority on the council, and the mayor swore an annual oath to the citizens. Fustian weaving and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a> trade made Ulm rich, and it acquired the largest territory of any imperial city after <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/free-imperial-city-of-nuremberg">Nuremberg</a>. Its citizens began the minster in 1377. Ulm led the Swabian city leagues and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swabian-league">Swabian League</a>, and turned Protestant in 1530 to 1531.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Ulm lost its freedom to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a> in 1802 and passed to Württemberg in 1810; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> defeated an Austrian army there in 1805. The minster spire was completed in 1890, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albert-einstein">Albert Einstein</a> was born in Ulm in 1879.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/free-imperial-city-of-ulm.jpg" alt="Free Imperial City of Ulm" width="320" height="185"><figcaption>Image: Spuner, Menke Atlas, 1880 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Reichsstadt_Ulm_1789.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1274 CE – 1802 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>imperial city</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Ulm</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Lutheranism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Swabian German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Ulm</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1538&amp;focus=free-imperial-city-of-ulm" aria-label="Free Imperial City of Ulm on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4912 485 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Free Imperial City of Ulm lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5232 741L5229 694M5232 741L5269 749M5232 741L5212 730"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5229 694h0M5269 749h0M5212 730h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5232 741h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5232 741h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Ulm. <a href="/atlas?year=1538&amp;focus=free-imperial-city-of-ulm">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
